import * from os;
import * from io_win;

mod export io;

// DOCS -- public `io` module (UNIFIED_CORE_LIBRARY.md Phase 2). This is the
// unified, portable front of the standard-input library. It contains ZERO
// win32 imports: every kernel32 call moved verbatim into the internal
// Windows backend std/io_win.sr, and the DLL-backed entry below routes
// through the OS gate `if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS)` before touching it.
//
// The gate uses osType() (std/os.sr) instead of the native `os.type` query
// because the self-hosted backend's builtin `os` dispatch covers only
// os.name/os.version/os.build today -- `os.type` miscompiles there in both
// spellings (property access yields a null string, the call form falls into
// builtin dispatch and returns garbage). osType() is plain Silver: it
// returns the OSTypes.WINDOWS contract value on every implementation without
// needing native support, and it can be switched to the native query once
// mini_cg learns a `type` branch.
//
// The else branch of the gate is unreachable on this backend (silverc only
// emits Windows PEs and osType() is the compile-time WINDOWS constant). For
// a future non-Windows backend the fallback follows the module's existing
// failure style: readLine returns "" -- the same value the pre-split body
// returned when the stdin handle was invalid. The remaining entries are NOT
// gated on purpose: the input() alias and the prompt variant are pure Silver
// delegation through readLine, so they inherit the gate through that call
// and keep their original shape (printline is a builtin, not a DLL call).
//
// DISPATCH NOTE (per compiler, unchanged by this split): the self-hosted
// compiler intercepts plain `input` and `readLine` calls with its native
// genInputCall builtin (mini_cg: GetStdHandle(-10) + one ReadFile into a
// 4096-byte heap buffer) before this module's globals are consulted, so the
// wrapper's readLine body below is reached there only via `io.readLine()`
// member calls -- the receiver "io" is not a builtin-module receiver, so
// those fall through to this file exactly as they hit the pre-split
// implementation. silverc-rs has no native readLine and its native `input`
// stub (codegen.rs gen_input) only fires when no user-defined `input` is in
// scope: once `import * from io;` resolves (through the exe-ancestor
// silverc/std walk; io is not in the rs builtin_modules_set), io's own
// functions win the func_labels dispatch, and the wrapper forwards into the
// internal backend. Both paths produce the same behavior as the pre-split
// module.

public string readLine() {
    if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS) {
        return ioWinReadLine();
    }
    return "";
}

public string input() << readLine();

public string input(string prompt) {
    printline(prompt);
    return readLine();
}
